#* {{quote-book|en|1731|w:Thomas Bayes|Divine Benevolence|https://archive.org/details/DivineBenevolenceOrAnAttemptToProveThatThe/Bayes-DivineBenevolence-1731-BWOrig/page/n71/mode/2up|73|For the moſt '''happy''' univerſe is not one that conſiſts of the greateſt poſſible number of the moſt '''happy''' beings only&nbsp;;{{…}}|location=w:London}}
#* {{RQ:Pope Essay on Man|epistle=II|page=17|passage=Whate’er the ''Paſſion'', Knowledge, Fame, or [[pelf|Pelf]],<br>Not one will change his Neighbour with himſelf.<br>The Learn’d are '''happy''', Nature to explore&nbsp;;<br>The Fool is '''happy''', that he knows no more&nbsp;;<br>The Rich are '''happy''' in the plenty given&nbsp;;<br>The Poor contents him with the Care of Heaven.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|1807||tlr=William Bridgman|chapter=That the Happy Man has Need of Worthy Friends|The Paraphrase of an Anonymous Greek Writer, (hitherto Published Under the Name of Andronicus Rhodius) on the {{w|Nicomachean Ethics}} of {{w|Aristotle}}|location=w:London|publisher=w:Charles Whittingham||section=book IX|415|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=Jw_nAAAAMAAJ&pg=PA415|oclc=3057125|But since the '''happy''' are sufficient to themselves they have no need of friends; and hence it is said,{{pb}}“When Fortune’s goods abound, what boots a friend?“”{{pb}}Thus then it appears that the '''happy''' do not require friends.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|1829|w:Charles Knowlton|chapter=On the Passions|Elements of Modern Materialism: Inculcating the Idea of a Future State, in which All Will be More Happy, under whatever Circumstances They May be Placed than if They Experienced No Misery in this Life|location=w:[[Adams, Massachusetts|Adams]]|publisher=A. Oakey||324|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=mTM9AAAAYAAJ&pg=PA324|oclc=367405965|While they are in this state striving perhaps to render their fellow beings more '''happy''', of whatever sect or denomination they may be, they meet with one or more persons who undertake to convert their mere cold belief in religious doctrines—which is at best little better than mere morality—into real effective religion, a religion that will move the tongue.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|1901|Edith Goodyear Alger|chapter=Roy’s Birthday|A Primer of Work and Play|location=w:Boston|publisher=w:D. C. Heath and Company||49|pageurl=https://babel.hathitrust.org/cgi/imgsrv/download/pdf?id=inu.30000114218161;orient=0;size=100;seq=57;num=49;attachment=0|oclc=2885602|A '''happy''' birthday to you,<br>A '''happy''' birthday to you,<br>A '''happy''' birthday, dear Roy,<br>A '''happy''' birthday to you.|footer=<small>''Note'': This is the first occurrence in print of the lyrics of the song “{{w|Happy Birthday to You}}”.</small>}}
#* {{quote-book|en|1990|Peter Woods|chapter=Laughing at School|The '''Happiest''' Days?: How Pupils Cope With Schools|location=w:Basingstoke|publisher=The Falmer Press, {{w|Taylor & Francis}}||182|isbn=9781850007302|In different ways, therefore, for many pupils, whether they benefit greatly from the system, or just 'get by', or are 'bored stiff' by the lessons, or for the most part are completely rebellious, schooldays do often appear to be 'the '''happiest''' days'.}}
#* {{quote-song|en|author=w:Pharrell Williams|title=w:[[Happy (Pharrell Williams song)|Happy]]|album=w:[[Girl (Pharrell Williams album)|Girl]]|artist=w:Pharrell Williams|date=2013-11-21|passage=Because I’m '''happy'''<br>Clap along if you know what happiness is to you<br>Because I’m '''happy'''<br>Clap along if you feel like that’s what you wanna do}}
#* {{quote-book|en|2014|Erica Brown|chapter=Pondering the Afterlife|Happier Endings: A Meditation on Life and Death|location=w:New York|publisher=w:Simon & Schuster||49|isbn=9781451649222|People who believe that a better life awaits us after this one would appear to have secured a '''happier''' ending, even before they come close to the end. There is little to be afraid of if you're armed with the promise of a wonderful future. Death is only a portal to greater joy.}}
